@*
    Where more SAPI5 voices come from, on CH4 and CH5 both, from one source so the two can't drift.

    Deliberately not a "download voices" button. There is no link that reliably yields a voice
    DCS-SR-ExternalAudio.exe can see: it speaks through SAPI5, and what Windows installs these days
    are OneCore voices, which SAPI5 usually cannot enumerate. A button pointing at the Microsoft
    download pages would mostly produce voices that look installed, get typed into the field, and
    then fail silently - no audio, no error. So this says what actually works, cheapest first, and
    names the risk of the two routes that carry one.

    The routes are ordered by what they cost you if they go wrong, not by how good they sound.
*@

<details class="voice-sources">
    <summary>Where do I get more voices?</summary>

    <div class="route">
        <strong>1. A Windows language pack</strong> — Settings → Time &amp; language → Language &amp;
        region → add a language, then its speech features. The free, reversible one, so try it first.
        It is a coin toss, though: the older <em>"… Desktop"</em> voices (<code>Microsoft Hedda
        Desktop</code>) do appear to SAPI5, the modern "natural" ones don't. Install, press
        <strong>List available voices</strong>, and believe that list rather than the Windows
        settings.
    </div>

    <div class="route">
        <strong>2. Copy a voice into the SAPI5 registry branch</strong> — the voices Windows hides
        from SAPI5 sit under
        <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Speech_OneCore\Voices\Tokens</code>. Export the one you want,
        change <code>Speech_OneCore</code> to <code>Speech</code> in the exported file, and merge it
        back. Back up the registry first. If your ExternalAudio build is 32-bit, it reads
        <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\SPEECH\Voices\Tokens</code> instead, so the entries
        have to go there as well — the surest way to find out which is to do one and check whether
        the voice shows up in the list.
    </div>

    <div class="route warn">
        <strong>3. NaturalVoiceSAPIAdapter</strong> — a third-party SAPI5 engine that hands the
        Windows 11 Narrator and Azure voices to any SAPI5 program, Darkstar included. It is the only
        route that gets you the genuinely good voices without leaving SAPI, and its own author calls
        it <em>"more like a hack than a proper solution"</em>: it uses keys extracted from system
        files, a Windows update can break it, and the newest Narrator voices already don't work with
        it. Install the 32-bit and 64-bit versions both, since which one matters depends on
        ExternalAudio.
        <a href="https://github.com/gexgd0419/NaturalVoiceSAPIAdapter" target="_blank">github.com/gexgd0419/NaturalVoiceSAPIAdapter</a>
    </div>

    <div class="route">
        <strong>Or skip SAPI entirely.</strong> Azure AI Speech and Google Cloud TTS already work
        through <code>ExternalAudioExtraArgs</code> on CH5 — far better voices, charged per
        character, and the provider's voice names go straight into the voice fields even though they
        never appear in the list.
    </div>
</details>
